course: adult-life
lesson: 14
title: Grown-ups who argue and make up
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grown-ups who love each other still disagree, sometimes loudly, because two people sharing a home, money and children will not always want the same things.
explain: Most arguments end, people say sorry in words or with a kind gesture, and life goes back to normal; when things stay hard for a while, couples may get help from a counsellor.
explain: Picture a house in Melbourne: you hear raised voices downstairs after bedtime, next morning Mum says "Tell your father his breakfast's ready", and that evening Dad walks in with flowers and an awkward grin.
explain: None of this is a child's job to fix, and none of it is a child's fault; you are allowed to say you felt worried, and to ask "Is everything okay?"
fact: An anniversary is the yearly date of when a couple married or got together, so a "15th Anniversary" card means fifteen years.
fact: Raised voices downstairs after bedtime usually mean parents are arguing, and they often wait until children are in bed so they will not get upset.
fact: "Not in front of the children" means the grown-ups need to talk about something serious and want to protect you from worry, not that you did something wrong.
fact: "Tell your father his dinner's ready" is the silent treatment: someone so cross they will not speak to the other person directly.
fact: The silent treatment usually makes things worse, and children should not have to carry messages between upset grown-ups.
fact: Coming home with flowers the day after a row is making up, a way of saying sorry and wanting to be friends again.
fact: A rough patch is a hard time in a relationship, with more arguing or unhappiness, and many couples come through it.
fact: Disagreeing is normal; talking it through is how couples decide things together.
fact: A parent sleeping on the sofa for a few nights may mean a disagreement, or something simple like snoring or a bad back.
fact: "Going to see someone to help us talk" means visiting a counsellor, a trained person who helps couples understand each other, which is a sensible, hopeful step.
fact: Grown-ups in love show affection with hugs and kisses, and it is normal for children to find it embarrassing.
fact: Grown-ups call very sweet romantic film scenes "soppy", and children are allowed to look away or roll their eyes.
exercise: A card says "Happy 15th Anniversary". What does it mean? | The couple married or got together fifteen years ago | Someone in the family is turning fifteen years old today | The couple have had fifteen arguments | The house is fifteen years old
exercise: How might a couple celebrate an anniversary? | With a card, flowers or a meal out | By moving house | By going back to school | By selling the wedding ring
exercise: You hear raised voices downstairs after bedtime. What is most likely happening? | Your parents are having an argument | A burglar is in the house, going through the drawers | The telly is being fixed | Your parents are singing a song
exercise: Why do parents often wait until children are in bed to argue? | So the children do not get upset | Because arguing is only allowed at night, after nine o'clock | Because they are too tired in the day | So the neighbours can hear better
exercise: Dad says "I'm not talking about this in front of the children." What does it mean? | It is a grown-up problem and they want to protect you from worry | You have done something very wrong and they are deciding on your punishment | They are planning a surprise party for you | They do not trust you to keep secrets
exercise: Mum says "Tell your father his dinner's ready." What is going on? | Mum is so cross she is not speaking to Dad directly | Mum has lost her voice | Dad cannot hear because he is far away at the bottom of the garden | Mum wants you to practise being a waiter
exercise: What can you say if you are asked to carry messages between cross grown-ups? | "Can you tell him yourself?" | "Only if you pay me" | Nothing, and change the message on the way | "He says he hates you"
exercise: Dad comes home with flowers the day after a row. Why? | He is saying sorry and making up | It is the anniversary by chance every time | He wants the house to smell nice for visitors | Mum asked for flowers before the row
exercise: Mum tells Gran "We're going through a rough patch." What does she mean? | Things are hard between her and Dad right now | The garden path has big bumps and holes in it | Mum's skin is dry in winter | They are on a bumpy road trip
exercise: Mum says "Your dad and I don't always agree, and that's normal." Why is disagreeing normal? | Two people who love each other can still have different opinions | It means they do not love each other any more and will soon split up | Married people are supposed to argue every day | Only parents who are splitting up disagree
exercise: Dad has slept on the sofa for a few nights. Which of these could be the reason? | A row, or something simple like snoring or a bad back | He has been told he cannot ever come upstairs again | The sofa is the only bed in the house | He is practising for a camping trip
exercise: "We're going to see someone to help us talk." Who is "someone"? | A counsellor who helps couples understand each other | A lawyer to arrange a divorce as quickly as possible for them | A teacher who gives grown-ups homework | A doctor to check their ears
exercise: Is going to a counsellor a good or a bad sign? | A good sign, it shows they care about the relationship | A bad sign, it means they have given up on each other for good | A bad sign, only very ill people go | Neither, counsellors only help children
exercise: Mum and Dad kiss in the kitchen and you shout "Ewww!" What is true? | It is normal to find it embarrassing at your age | Kissing in the kitchen is against the house rules for grown-ups | They are doing it to make you angry | Grown-ups only kiss when they are sorry
exercise: Dad says "Cover your eyes, it's the soppy part." What is the soppy part? | A very sweet romantic scene | A part with a lot of soap and bubbles in it | A frightening scene with monsters | The part where everyone cries over a pet
exercise: Is an argument a child's job to fix? | No, grown-ups' arguments are theirs to sort out | Yes, the oldest child must always fix it by talking to both | Yes, if the child is good at talking | No, the neighbours have to fix it
test: Your sister whispers at the top of the stairs: "They're shouting, it's 'cos I broke the vase, isn't it?" What is true? | No, grown-ups' arguments are not caused by a broken vase | Yes, broken vases are what most rows are about | Yes, so she should go down and say sorry right now before it gets worse | No, they are shouting because they are deaf
test: next morning it's all "pass the salt" "ask your mother". no one's talking to anyone. what is this | The silent treatment, they are still cross with each other | A game where nobody may speak until somebody laughs out loud | They both have sore throats | Breakfast always has to be silent
test: overheard Dad on phone: "Yeah, roses, the red ones... no, I was an idiot last night." What is he doing? | Getting flowers to make up after a row | Ordering red roses to plant in the garden | Booking a table for a party | Telling a friend about a film
test: Gran on the phone: "Oh love, every marriage has its rough patches, you'll get through it." What does Gran think? | Hard times between couples are common and can be got through | Mum and Dad must divorce straight away | The path to the house needs repairs | Mum is going on a long walk
test: Your friend says "my dad's been on the sofa all week, they're definitely getting divorced". Put him right. | Not always; it can be a row or something simple like snoring | Yes, sleeping on the sofa always means a divorce is on the way | Yes, but only if it is more than three nights | No, dads always sleep on sofas
test: Mum: "We're seeing a counsellor on Thursdays now." Your brother groans "great, they're broken". What is closer to the truth? | Getting help shows they want to make things better | Counsellors are only for couples who have already split up for good | It means one of them is in trouble with the police | It means they will stop talking at home
test: Dad: "Not now, love. Not in front of the kids." Your cousin says "means you're in trouble". Is she right? | No, it is a grown-up matter they want to talk about privately | Yes, it always means a child is in trouble and will be punished later tonight | Yes, and you will be grounded tonight | No, it means dinner is ready
test: you're asked: "go tell your mum she can get her own tea then". what's the best thing to say | "Can you tell her yourself?" | Shout the message even louder | Change it to something nice without asking | Refuse to speak to anyone ever again
test: The card on the fridge says "Happy 10th Anniversary". How long ago did they marry or get together? | Ten years ago | Ten months ago | One year ago | A hundred years ago
test: Mum and Dad hug and kiss in the hallway. Your little sister says "yuck, they must be getting married again". What is true? | Grown-ups in love just show affection with hugs and kisses | Kissing means they will have a second wedding next summer with all the family | They are pretending for a photo | Kissing in the hall means they had a row
test: "oh here we go, the soppy bit" Dad groans, as the film couple stare at each other. What kind of scene is it? | A sweet romantic scene | An exciting car chase | A sad scene about a pet | A funny scene with a clown
test: Mum says she and Dad disagree about where to go on holiday. Your brother asks "so do you hate each other now?" What does Mum most likely mean? | Disagreeing is normal and they will decide together | They have stopped loving each other because of the holiday | One of them will go on holiday alone forever | They will never go on holiday again
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a couple consulting a counsellor. | To be helped to understand one another and resolve disagreements | To obtain a decree of divorce | To be examined in respect of their hearing | To undertake a lengthy course of academic study at a university of their choosing
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the conveyance of a message such as "Tell your father his dinner is ready". | It is the silent treatment, which ordinarily aggravates a dispute | It constitutes a courteous invitation to supper | It constitutes evidence that the father is hard of hearing | It constitutes a game to be enjoyed by every member of the household
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises disagreement between persons who love one another? | It is normal, and discussion thereof is how decisions are reached | It is invariably a sign of imminent separation and eventual divorce | It occurs solely in households with children | It is prohibited between married persons
exam: It is given that a child overhears an argument between parents. Ascertain the responsibility of the child in respect thereof. | The child bears no responsibility to resolve it | The child is obliged to mediate forthwith | The child is the cause and must apologise | The child is obliged to choose a side
